  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1228 PM CDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nWV imagery indicates a northwest flow aloft prevailing across \nthe Western High Plains downstream of an upper level high \ncentered across the Desert Southwest. Near the surface, a lee \nside trough of low pressure extends northeast across southeast \nColorado into northwest Kansas.\n\nAnother round of minimal isolated thunderstorm chances (20%)\nexists across west central Kansas into extreme southwest Kansas\nthis evening with the SREF indicating little change in the \noverall pattern from yesterday with H5 vort maxima associated \nwith a weak shortwave ejecting eastward out of the northern \nColorado Rockies into the Western High Plains. Near the surface,\nprevailing southerlies will continue to support some moisture \nreturn across the area, providing minimal instability with \nMUCAPE values upward of 500 J/kg or so. With steepening mid-\nlevel lapse rates, isolated thunderstorm development cannot be \nruled out toward early evening as ejecting H5 vort maxima \ninteract with an axis of low level convergence associated with \nthe aforementioned trough extending from extreme southeast \nColorado into northwest Kansas. A few CAMs suggest this while \nthe HREF hints at it with 10% probability of 6-hr QPF exceeding \nonly 0.1 of an inch across west central Kansas into extreme \nsouthwest Kansas by late evening. A secondary focus will be more\ninto central Kansas later this evening, including the I-70 \ncorridor, as thunderstorms likely developing in a field of much \nhigher instability in northwest Kansas/southwest Nebraska track \neastward overnight. Similar conditions are forecast Monday into \nTuesday with little change to the larger scale pattern.\n\nVery warm temperatures are forecast again tonight as prevailing \nsoutherlies reinforce a warmer air mass across southwest/central \nKansas. Considering the HREF paints a widespread 70-90% probability \nof temperatures dropping below 80F, look for lows generally in the \n70s(F). With a persistent pattern in place, expect afternoon highs \nback up around 100 to 105F Monday and Tuesday.",
